= Knowledge — Simple =

'''Knowledge''' is more than simply thinking that something is true.

If you know something, it should actually be true, and your connection to that truth should not be only a lucky guess.

For example, suppose you guess that a coin will land heads and it does. Your [[Belief/Simple|belief]] was true, but you did not know the result before the coin landed just because your guess happened to be correct.

Philosophers often distinguish several forms of knowledge:

* '''knowing that''' — knowing a fact or proposition;
* '''knowing how''' — knowing how to do something;
* '''knowing by acquaintance''' — being familiar with a person, place, or experience.

A famous older idea said that knowledge is '''justified true belief''': the claim is true, you believe it, and you have a good reason for believing it.

But philosophers discovered examples where all three conditions seem to be present and the person is still right only because of luck. These are called '''Gettier cases'''.

So there is no single definition of knowledge that all philosophers accept.

Knowledge is closely related to [[Evidence/Simple|evidence]], [[Justification/Simple|justification]], and [[Epistemology/Simple|epistemology]].

